launch date, provider account or rollout was activated by the local implementation.

Migrations `0055_bounded_rank_reads` and `0056_bounded_retention` must precede the
matching API/worker rollout. The rank migration seeds exact public/all time
counts and compressed UUID tie trees from existing PBs while writers are
quiesced; rehearse its duration, locks and storage on a representative copy.
Tie trees store between N and 2N−1 nodes for N visible PBs, rather than one node
per UUID prefix. The retention migration adds indexed last-activity deadlines;
workers upgrade legacy NULL deadlines in bounded batches without changing
first-observed cohort dates. Verification admissions use independent transactional
slots as their exact shared-capacity fence and no longer count all reservations
in preflight. Before external object writes, preflight probes at most 64 randomly
sampled slot keys. A full queue rejects immediately; an occupied sample on a
larger queue returns retryable 503 even if an unsampled vacancy exists. With one
vacancy, a 64-slot sample succeeds with probability `64 / capacity`; size queues
from measured worker service capacity to avoid retry amplification near saturation.
The database trigger remains the exact admission fence. Slot allocation still
depends on the configured finite queue size;
measure it before materially increasing `BMT_MAX_QUEUED_RUNS`.

Public track validation always queues. `BMT_ALLOW_SYNC_TRACK_VALIDATION=true` is only for explicitly configured non-production tests/development. Current clients require discovery v3 and trajectory verification from bootstrap. Ordinary uploads use trajectory records; a service without those capabilities requires a matching update. Gzip and batched status remain capability-negotiated. Update API/worker together, then verify the client contract; no deployment is included in this task.

`python -m bmt_backend.maintenance --retention --apply-retention --online` uses shared publication pins and bounded resumable batches. Closed weeks archive after the retention interval, keeping scores and membership while releasing optional evidence; public PBs, creator proofs and immutable track versions stay protected. `--offline` remains an alternative with all writers stopped. Dry-run remains the default. Run another complete object scan after a changing-directory cleanup. Timestamped v2 retry keys expire; legacy keys and permanent fulfillment/grant identities remain protected.

Local object pagination prepares one disk-backed manifest before opening maintenance database transactions. This first step visits the directories once (O(n) time, bounded memory); subsequent batches seek directly to a byte offset and inspect at most 1,000 candidates. Preparation may take time on a large volume and requires roughly 73 bytes of temporary disk space per ordinary object per active scan. Startup cleanup retains its independent 1,000-entry cap and does not prepare a manifest. Pass `next_object_cursor` back through `--object-cursor` to continue across process restarts. Deletion does not require adjusting the cursor. Old `{shard,offset}` cursors safely start one fresh scan. Manifests live in `.object-scans-v1`, expire after seven days, and allow at most four unfinished scans per volume; finish an existing scan instead of repeatedly starting another. Completed manifests remain retryable until a new scan reclaims them. An expired/missing cursor requires restarting without a cursor. Interrupted preparation is rebuilt on retry. New or concurrently changing objects may wait for the next full scan; current file age/type, references, active upload guards and publication pins are rechecked before deletion. Portable object backups exclude these rebuildable manifests. GCS/S3 continuation tokens and object paths are unchanged.

Preparation collects candidate names without per-entry type queries; the current regular-file and directory-link checks run when each page is consumed. Startup requests metadata only for temporary-upload candidates within its 1,000-entry enumeration allowance. Manifest writes use one bounded 256 KiB buffer. Pages use the immutable manifest's known length instead of explicitly reading ahead to test for another page. These reduce work while retaining the existing cursor format, manifest byte layout, reference checks and upload guards. They do not remove the initial linear scan or its temporary disk requirement.

`backup.sh` defaults to `BMT_BACKUP_MODE=online`. It acquires the shared GC guard before `pg_dump`, streams immutable objects, writes checksums/completion, then releases the guard. A crash leaves GC paused, while API and workers continue. Recover using the private `GC_RECOVERY_TOKEN` with `python -m bmt_backend.backup_guard end --token <saved-token>`; the token must match the active guard. The completion marker is written after successful cleanup; guard-release or writer-restart failures keep it absent. Rehearse restore into a different empty project with the matching image/schema before switching traffic. The task tested pg_dump/archive/restore with live synthetic writes; the complete Compose wrapper needs target-host acceptance.

The optional `compose.cloud.yaml` selects an explicitly configured private HTTPS S3-compatible endpoint, bucket/prefix and credentials. Use it only after provider integration, access controls and backup/restore have been tested. `BMT_COMPOSE_CLOUD=1` adds that override to backup/restore helper commands. Provider backups stream bounded verified objects into the same portable archive; restore to a fresh **local** object volume (`BMT_COMPOSE_CLOUD=0`) first, verify hashes/PBs, then rehearse a separate verified transfer to the chosen provider before enabling its runtime configuration. Credentials are supplied privately and never included in artifacts. Default NAS configuration retains the local adapter and isolated worker network.

## Exception recovery rollout

Migrate a backed-up database through `0015_exception_recovery` before matching API/worker/client rollout. See [repair policies and actual tests](../../docs/BACKEND_EXCEPTION_FIXES_2026_10_05.md). This task did not deploy/restart existing services.

`BMT_TRANSPORT_PROFILE=compact` is the only accepted transport profile. The retired `legacy` value is rejected at startup. Deploy the matching API/client and migration `0062_retire_discovery_cache` with its 0060/0061 prerequisites. Bootstrap must advertise `submission_recovery_version: 1`: before resending a pre-update job, the client privately reads `/v1/run-tickets/{ticket_id}/submission` and retains an already accepted result without changing its body or key. Unaccepted input-only saved jobs use a bounded canonical-input recovery path; ordinary race submissions remain trajectory-only. A lost recovery reply or conflicting older in-flight body is reconciled by the same ticket lookup. Original recorded files and operation keys survive recovery. If that capability is absent, the original job stays pending until the service is updated. Retain compatible original worker cohorts until their accepted jobs drain; claims now filter actual pinned build/config. `BMT_WORKER_CONTRACT_WAIT_SECONDS=7200` bounds absent-compatible-capacity waiting without spending verification attempts; evidence stays retryable with its original contract. `BMT_REFRESH_RECOVERY_SECONDS=120` (maximum 300) bounds private same-operation refresh retransmission; distinct-operation/legacy reuse still revokes. Trusted proxy configuration, readiness/worker health and synthetic verification remain necessary.

Resume recovery never replays an abandoned charge. It permanently closes an unsubmitted attempt before refunding its requested unsettled banked/same-day resource. Submitted attempts cannot refund; daily allowance never rolls over and ad_preview never mints banked rewards. Bounded old-unsettled retention returns eligible banked resources before pruning. No ad/store fulfillment configuration was added.
